      "side": 1,
      "message": "The approach is right: a small main driving ToolFactory.createCodeFormatter with the checked-in Eclipse profile is the only clean way to format JGit\u0027s style headlessly. Eclipse JDT ships no plain-jar CLI (the org.eclipse.jdt.core.JavaCodeFormatter application needs the Equinox/OSGi runtime and has no check mode), and google-java-format / spring-javaformat enforce their own styles, so neither can apply eclipse-JGit-Format.xml.\n\nMy concern is size. The ~1000 LoC is almost entirely the --changed-lines feature, not JDT formatting: driving the formatter is ~80 LoC. GitDiff.java (187) exists solely for changed-lines, and about half of JdtFormatter.java is the partial-range machinery.\n\nNone does changed-line scoping — revelc formatter-maven-plugin, spring javaformat, algodesigner/eclipse-format, and the Eclipse headless app all format whole files. The minimal ones (revelc, eclipse-format) are thin wrappers over ToolFactory.createCodeFormatter driven by the profile XML. spring-javaformat is Eclipse-based too but customizes the formatter and fixes its conventions, so it can\u0027t apply eclipse-JGit-Format.xml — same disqualifier as google-java-format.\n\nSuggestion: ship v1 as whole-file formatting of explicitly named files (what tools/gjf.sh does in gerrit), drop GitDiff and --changed-lines, and reuse args4j for parsing. Same capability in ~150 LoC. Add changed-line scoping later if it is actually needed.",
      "revId": "bba38a2b9c5a73bd14c8fc4ccafe343a0ca72a41",
